NNAAMMEE
     eeqqnn — eqn language reference for mandoc

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The eeqqnn language is an equation-formatting language.  It is used within
     mdoc(7) and man(7) UNIX manual pages.  It describes the _s_t_r_u_c_t_u_r_e of an
     equation, not its mathematical meaning.  This manual describes the eeqqnn
     language accepted by the mandoc(1) utility, which corresponds to the Sec‐
     ond Edition eeqqnn specification (see _S_E_E _A_L_S_O for references).

     An equation starts with an input line containing exactly the characters
     ‘.EQ’, may contain multiple input lines, and ends with an input line con‐
     taining exactly the characters ‘.EN’.  Equivalently, an equation can be
     given in the middle of a single text input line by surrounding it with
     the equation delimiters defined with the ddeelliimm statement.

     The equation grammar is as follows, where quoted strings are case-sensi‐
     tive literals in the input:

           eqn     : box | eqn box
           box     : text
                   | "{" eqn "}"
                   | "define" text text
                   | "ndefine" text text
                   | "tdefine" text text
                   | "gfont" text
                   | "gsize" text
                   | "set" text text
                   | "undef" text
                   | "sqrt" box
                   | box pos box
                   | box mark
                   | "matrix" "{" [col "{" list "}"]* "}"
                   | pile "{" list "}"
                   | font box
                   | "size" text box
                   | "left" text eqn ["right" text]
           col     : "lcol" | "rcol" | "ccol" | "col"
           text    : [^space\"]+ | \".*\"
           pile    : "lpile" | "cpile" | "rpile" | "pile"
           pos     : "over" | "sup" | "sub" | "to" | "from"
           mark    : "dot" | "dotdot" | "hat" | "tilde" | "vec"
                   | "dyad" | "bar" | "under"
           font    : "roman" | "italic" | "bold" | "fat"
           list    : eqn
                   | list "above" eqn
           space   : [\^~ \t]

     White-space consists of the space, tab, circumflex, and tilde characters.
     It is required to delimit tokens consisting of alphabetic characters and
     it is ignored at other places.  Braces and quotes also delimit tokens.
     If within a quoted string, these space characters are retained.  Quoted
     strings are also not scanned for keywords, glyph names, and expansion of
     definitions.  To print a literal quote character, it can be prepended
     with a backslash or expressed with the \(dq escape sequence.

     Subequations can be enclosed in braces to pass them as arguments to oper‐
     ation keywords, overriding standard operation precedence.  Braces can be
     nested.  To set a brace verbatim, it needs to be enclosed in quotes.

     The following text terms are translated into a rendered glyph, if avail‐
     able: alpha, beta, chi, delta, epsilon, eta, gamma, iota, kappa, lambda,
     mu, nu, omega, omicron, phi, pi, psi, rho, sigma, tau, theta, upsilon,
     xi, zeta, DELTA, GAMMA, LAMBDA, OMEGA, PHI, PI, PSI, SIGMA, THETA, UP‐
     SILON, XI, inter (intersection), union (union), prod (product), int (in‐
     tegral), sum (summation), grad (gradient), del (vector differential),
     times (multiply), cdot (center-dot), nothing (zero-width space), approx
     (approximately equals), prime (prime), half (one-half), partial (partial
     differential), inf (infinity), >> (much greater), << (much less), <-
     (left arrow), -> (right arrow), +- (plus-minus), != (not equal), ==
     (equivalence), <= (less-than-equal), and >= (more-than-equal).  The char‐
     acter escape sequences documented in mandoc_char(7) can be used, too.

     The following control statements are available:

     ddeeffiinnee  Replace all occurrences of a key with a value.  Its syntax is as
             follows:

                   ddeeffiinnee _k_e_y _c_v_a_l_c

             The first character of the value string, _c, is used as the delim‐
             iter for the value _v_a_l.  This allows for arbitrary enclosure of
             terms (not just quotes), such as

                   ddeeffiinnee _f_o_o _'_b_a_r _b_a_z_'
                   ddeeffiinnee _f_o_o _c_b_a_r _b_a_z_c

             It is an error to have an empty _k_e_y or _v_a_l.  Note that a quoted
             _k_e_y causes errors in some eeqqnn implementations and should not be
             considered portable.  It is not expanded for replacements.  Defi‐
             nitions may refer to other definitions; these are evaluated re‐
             cursively when text replacement occurs and not when the defini‐
             tion is created.

             Definitions can create arbitrary strings, for example, the fol‐
             lowing is a legal construction.

                   define foo 'define'
                   foo bar 'baz'

             Self-referencing definitions will raise an error.  The nnddeeffiinnee
             statement is a synonym for ddeeffiinnee, while ttddeeffiinnee is discarded.

     ddeelliimm   This statement takes a string argument consisting of two bytes,
             to be used as the opening and closing delimiters for equations in
             the middle of text input lines.  Conventionally, the dollar sign
             is used for both delimiters, as follows:

                   .EQ
                   delim $$
                   .EN
                   An equation like $sin pi = 0$ can now be entered
                   in the middle of a text input line.

             The special statement ddeelliimm ooffff temporarily disables previously
             declared delimiters and ddeelliimm oonn reenables them.

     ggffoonntt   Set the default font of subsequent output.  Its syntax is as fol‐
             lows:

                   ggffoonntt _f_o_n_t

             In mandoc, this value is discarded.

     ggssiizzee   Set the default size of subsequent output.  Its syntax is as fol‐
             lows:

                   ggssiizzee [+|-]_s_i_z_e

             The _s_i_z_e value should be an integer.  If prepended by a sign, the
             font size is changed relative to the current size.

     sseett     Set an equation mode.  In mandoc, both arguments are thrown away.
             Its syntax is as follows:

                   sseett _k_e_y _v_a_l

             The _k_e_y and _v_a_l are not expanded for replacements.  This state‐
             ment is a GNU extension.

     uunnddeeff   Unset a previously-defined key.  Its syntax is as follows:

                   ddeeffiinnee _k_e_y

             Once invoked, the definition for _k_e_y is discarded.  The _k_e_y is
             not expanded for replacements.  This statement is a GNU exten‐
             sion.

     Operation keywords have the following semantics:

     aabboovvee   See ppiillee.

     bbaarr     Draw a line over the preceding box.

     bboolldd    Set the following box using bold font.

     ccccooll    Like ccppiillee, but for use in mmaattrriixx.

     ccppiillee   Like ppiillee, but with slightly increased vertical spacing.

     ddoott     Set a single dot over the preceding box.

     ddoottddoott  Set two dots (dieresis) over the preceding box.

     ddyyaadd    Set a dyad symbol (left-right arrow) over the preceding box.

     ffaatt     A synonym for bboolldd.

     ffoonntt    Set the second argument using the font specified by the first ar‐
             gument; currently not recognized by the mandoc(1) eeqqnn parser.

     ffrroomm    Set the following box below the preceding box, using a slightly
             smaller font.  Used for sums, integrals, limits, and the like.

     hhaatt     Set a hat (circumflex) over the preceding box.

     iittaalliicc  Set the following box using italic font.

     llccooll    Like llppiillee, but for use in mmaattrriixx.

     lleefftt    Set the first argument as a big left delimiter before the second
             argument.  As an optional third argument, rriigghhtt can follow.  In
             that case, the fourth argument is set as a big right delimiter
             after the second argument.

     llppiillee   Like ccppiillee, but subequations are left-justified.

     mmaattrriixx  Followed by a list of columns enclosed in braces.  All columns
             need to have the same number of subequations.  The columns are
             set as a matrix.  The difference compared to multiple subsequent
             ppiillee operators is that in a mmaattrriixx, corresponding subequations in
             all columns line up horizontally, while each ppiillee does vertical
             spacing independently.

     oovveerr    Set a fraction.  The preceding box is the numerator, the follow‐
             ing box is the denominator.

     ppiillee    Followed by a list of subequations enclosed in braces, the sube‐
             quations being separated by aabboovvee keywords.  Sets the subequa‐
             tions one above the other, each of them centered.  Typically used
             to represent vectors in coordinate representation.

     rrccooll    Like rrppiillee, but for use in mmaattrriixx.

     rriigghhtt   See lleefftt; rriigghhtt cannot be used without lleefftt.  To set a big right
             delimiter without a big left delimiter, the following construc‐
             tion can be used:

                   lleefftt "" _b_o_x rriigghhtt _d_e_l_i_m_i_t_e_r

     rroommaann   Set the following box using the default font.

     rrppiillee   Like ccppiillee, but subequations are right-justified.

     ssiizzee    Set the second argument with the font size specified by the first
             argument; currently ignored by mandoc(1).  By prepending a plus
             or minus sign to the first argument, the font size can be se‐
             lected relative to the current size.

     ssqqrrtt    Set the square root of the following box.

     ssuubb     Set the following box as a subscript to the preceding box.

     ssuupp     Set the following box as a superscript to the preceding box.  As
             a special case, if a ssuupp clause immediately follows a ssuubb clause
             as in

                   _m_a_i_n_b_o_x ssuubb _s_u_b_b_o_x ssuupp _s_u_p_b_o_x

             both are set with respect to the same _m_a_i_n_b_o_x, that is, _s_u_p_b_o_x is
             set above _s_u_b_b_o_x.

     ttiillddee   Set a tilde over the preceding box.

     ttoo      Set the following box above the preceding box, using a slightly
             smaller font.  Used for sums and integrals and the like.  As a
             special case, if a ttoo clause immediately follows a ffrroomm clause as
             in

                   _m_a_i_n_b_o_x ffrroomm _f_r_o_m_b_o_x ttoo _t_o_b_o_x

             both are set below and above the same _m_a_i_n_b_o_x.

     uunnddeerr   Underline the preceding box.

     vveecc     Set a vector symbol (right arrow) over the preceding box.

     The binary operations ffrroomm, ttoo, ssuubb, and ssuupp group to the right, that is,

           _m_a_i_n_b_o_x ssuupp _s_u_p_b_o_x ssuubb _s_u_b_b_o_x

     is the same as

           _m_a_i_n_b_o_x ssuupp {_s_u_p_b_o_x ssuubb _s_u_b_b_o_x}

     and different from

           {_m_a_i_n_b_o_x ssuupp _s_u_p_b_o_x} ssuubb _s_u_b_b_o_x.

     By contrast, oovveerr groups to the left.

     In the following list, earlier operations bind more tightly than later
     operations:

     1.   ddyyaadd, vveecc, uunnddeerr, bbaarr, ttiillddee, hhaatt, ddoott, ddoottddoott
     2.   ffaatt, rroommaann, iittaalliicc, bboolldd, ssiizzee
     3.   ssuubb, ssuupp
     4.   ssqqrrtt
     5.   oovveerr
     6.   ffrroomm, ttoo

CCOOMMPPAATTIIBBIILLIITTYY
     This section documents the compatibility of mandoc eeqqnn and the troff eeqqnn
     implementation (including GNU troff).

     --   The text string ‘\"’ is interpreted as a literal quote in troff.  In
         mandoc, this is interpreted as a comment.
     --   In troff, The circumflex and tilde white-space symbols map to fixed-
         width spaces.  In mandoc, these characters are synonyms for the space
         character.
     --   The troff implementation of eeqqnn allows for equation alignment with
         the mmaarrkk and lliinneeuupp tokens.  mandoc discards these tokens.  The bbaacckk
         _n, ffwwdd _n, uupp _n, and ddoowwnn _n commands are also ignored.

